uci -q batch << EOI
# Keep OpenWrt's packet-steering service out of the NSS data path: its model is a
# host NAPI per hardware queue, which does not describe a datapath where the
# firmware delivers through N2H. In this mode the service writes rps_cpus=0 on the
# netdevs it owns (the Wi-Fi VAPs and the EDMA conduit); the cores-1-3 mask for
# the delivery netdevs comes from net.core.rps_default_mask in
# 12-nss-networking.conf.
#
# Measured on a Wi-Fi client to wired host flow through the router, two runs
# each: 749 and 800 Mbit/s with the service disabled against 619 and 626 with
# it at its default, and the default also produced n2h_rx_queue drops (158 and
# 83) where disabling it produced none. See 12-nss-networking.conf.
#
# Kernel flow offloading must be off as well: ECM owns acceleration. OpenWrt
# ships it off, but a config carried over from another build (sysupgrade keeps
# settings, and turning it on is the usual advice for a router without a
# hardware fast path) brings it along, and then the nft flowtable forwards
# every established TCP flow from its ingress hook before the netfilter hooks
# ECM classifies from. ECM never sees the flow, the firmware exceptions every
# packet of it to the host, and nothing in the logs says why. Measured on an
# Archer AX55 v1 moved over from a plain build: 2 rules for 138 connections,
# 32-64 % CPU on a Wi-Fi to WAN iperf3; the same flows at 3-8 % once it was
# off. uci-defaults run after the restored config is in place, so this is the
# right moment to clear it.
set network.globals.packet_steering='0'
set firewall.@defaults[0].flow_offloading='0'
set firewall.@defaults[0].flow_offloading_hw='0'
EOI

exit 0
